course: dictionary
lesson: 12626
title: Feelings in stories
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how a character feels without saying it outright. Dismay is one of them. When you meet it, look at what has just happened. «Sam had saved his pocket money for weeks, but the shop had sold out.» Sam's pocket money was wasted, so dismay must be a sad, sinking feeling.
explain: Dismay can be a noun, a verb or an adjective. As a noun it is a feeling: «Mia looked up in dismay.» As a verb, to dismay someone means to shock and upset them: «The rain dismayed the campers.» As an adjective, «The dismayed fans sat down.» In each case the clues are bad news or a disappointment.
explain: In formal writing you may see «to my dismay» or «to the dismay of». «To my dismay, the library was closed.» It means the writer was upset that the library was closed. Read on past the phrase, and you will find out what caused the upset.
fact: Dismay means a sudden sad, worried feeling when something bad or disappointing happens.
fact: To dismay someone is to make them feel shocked and upset.
fact: «To my dismay» means «and I was upset that».
fact: Dismay is a noun you cannot count: say «with dismay», not «a dismay».
fact: Useful phrases: «in dismay», «filled with dismay», «dismayed by».
exercise: «To my dismay, the library was closed.» What does dismay mean here? | A loud noise | Great happiness | A long wait | *A sudden sad, worried feeling
exercise: «Mia was dismayed when her kite snapped in the wind.» What does dismayed mean? | Very excited | *Shocked and upset | Sleepy and calm | Proud of herself
exercise: «The news of the flood filled the village with dismay.» Find and copy the word that tells you the news was bad. | *flood | filled | news | village
test: «Dad heard the match was cancelled, and his face showed dismay.» What does this tell you about Dad? | *Unhappy shock | Tiredness | Joy | Excitement about a party
test: Which phrase is formal and means «and the fans were upset that»? | *To the dismay of the fans | At the fans' party | The fans, dismay in hand | Fans in dismay of
exam: Which word best completes this sentence? «The class looked at the test results with ___.» | giggles | wonder | sleep | *dismay
